{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His early life involved captivity after the Battle of Inab yet he eventually secured his inheritance and ruled with a pragmatic approach that often favored diplomacy over reckless warfare. *Early Life and Captivity* describes how young Raymond faced immense challenges when his father died and he was taken prisoner by Nur ad-Din forces while still a child so he spent roughly ten years in Aleppo before ransom payments secured his freedom. This long imprisonment shaped his worldview and made him deeply cautious about engaging in military conflicts without clear advantages or sufficient resources to guarantee victory.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles* outlines the period when Raymond acted as regent for Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not always lead armies or manage state affairs effectively. During this time Raymond clashed with other noble factions such as the Courtenay family and later Guy of Lusignan because he believed their aggressive policies would provoke Saladin into launching a full-scale invasion of the Christian territories. He sought truces and negotiated treaties to maintain peace but his rivals accused him of cowardice or even treason due to his willingness to treat with Muslim leaders rather than fight them constantly on the battlefield. His political maneuvering aimed to preserve the kingdom yet it also created deep divisions among the Crusader nobility that weakened their unity against external threats.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy* details the catastrophic defeat where Raymond commanded a contingent of troops but survived the disaster that destroyed the main Crusader army and led to the fall of Jerusalem. He had warned against marching across arid lands without water supplies yet King Guy ignored this advice and moved the force into a trap set by Saladin near the Horns of Hattin. Raymond escaped the encirclement while many others perished or were captured including the True Cross relic that held great spiritual significance for the Christians. His later days were spent trying to rebuild defenses yet he died shortly after the battle leaving a complex legacy as a skilled diplomat whose warnings went unheeded by those who preferred martial glory over strategic caution. Historians view him as one of the most capable Latin leaders in the Levant during the twelfth century because he understood the limitations of Crusader power better than his contemporaries did.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -7.3910032e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.00071974663}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.047020264}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.027729942}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -8.3446537e-07}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -3.6954948e-06}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.0728842e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -5.960466e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -3.9339143e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.0006998842}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.05247744}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.006429015}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token":